# Ubuntu 26.10 (stonking) package names, one per line. The oaxaca-desktop meta
# package's Depends are generated from this file. Every name below was checked
# against the stonking Packages indexes on 2026-10-10.
alsa-utils
avahi-daemon
libnss-mdns
bash-completion
bat
bluez
bluez-tools
bolt
brightnessctl
cups
cups-filters
cups-pk-helper
ddcutil
dosfstools
exfatprogs
fastfetch
fcitx5
# flatpak: the Flathub store, preconfigured at install (install/config/flatpak.sh), decided 2026-10-10
flatpak
fcitx5-frontend-gtk3
fcitx5-frontend-gtk4
fcitx5-frontend-qt6
fd-find
fontconfig
foot
fzf
git
gnome-themes-extra
grim
gpu-screen-recorder-cli
gum
gvfs-backends
gvfs-fuse
hyprland-guiutils
hyprpicker
hyprsunset
imagemagick
imv
inotify-tools
network-manager
jq
less
libvips-tools
man-db
mpv
mpv-mpris
# nano: the default editor (oaxaca-launch-editor falls back to it when no editor is chosen),
# and what $EDITOR, git and sudoedit open; Ubuntu's ubuntu-standard only recommends it and the image
# has neither metapackage (2026-10-14)
nano
# papers: the document viewer and the PDF default (default/applications/mimeapps.list); Ubuntu's own
# default viewer (ubuntu-desktop-minimal recommends it) and GNOME's successor to Omarchy's evince,
# which stonking carries only as an alpha in universe (2026-10-14)
papers
# neovim: Ubuntu's, without Omarchy's LazyVim configuration (omarchy-nvim builds its plugin cache
# with network access, which this distro's package builds do not have); 2026-10-14
neovim
nautilus
python3-nautilus
fonts-noto-core
fonts-noto-cjk
fonts-noto-color-emoji
plocate
# plymouth: the boot splash; the settings package registers its theme with update-alternatives (step 7)
plymouth
# plymouth-label: the label plugin (label.so) that renders text for script themes; Arch's plymouth
# package builds it in, Ubuntu splits it out. The Omarchy theme draws text with Image.Text(), and
# without the plugin Plymouth's console viewer is never created and the script plugin of Ubuntu's
# 24.004.60 snapshot crashes in script_lib_sprite_refresh (seen booting the live ISO on the Legion
# laptop, 2026-10-11; fixed upstream on 2026-08-06 by a null check the snapshot predates).
plymouth-label
# pulseaudio-utils: pactl, which omarchy-audio-output-volume (the volume keys) and the other
# audio commands call against PipeWire's PulseAudio server; Arch's libpulse ships pactl, Ubuntu
# puts the client tools in this package (volume keys did nothing in the live session, 2026-10-11).
pulseaudio-utils
# gawk: Omarchy's scripts are written for GNU awk (omarchy-menu-keybindings uses the
# three-argument match()); Ubuntu's default awk is mawk, which rejects that syntax, so the
# keybindings menu listed nothing (live session, 2026-10-11). Installing gawk makes it the
# system awk through the alternatives system (priority above mawk's).
gawk
# libgtk-3-bin: gtk-launch, which the shell's application menu uses to start a desktop entry
# ("uwsm-app -- gtk-launch <id>.desktop", shell/services/AppLibrary.qml); Arch's gtk3 package
# ships it, Ubuntu splits it out. Without it nothing launched from the menu (2026-10-11).
libgtk-3-bin
# thermald, intel-lpmd, lsp-plugins-lv2: what install/hardware/*.sh add with omarchy-pkg-add on
# matching hardware (Intel laptops on battery, the speaker-tuning table). Shipped in the image so
# that an install without a network does not fail at those steps: omarchy-pkg-add skips apt for a
# package that is already installed, and the daemons stay inert where the hardware is absent
# (Legion laptop, 2026-10-11: "Package 'thermald' has no installation candidate" offline).
thermald
intel-lpmd
lsp-plugins-lv2
power-profiles-daemon
# upower: the shell's power panel and battery widget read UPower (Quickshell.Services.UPower);
# Arch pulls it in through quickshell, Ubuntu's quickshell does not depend on it.
upower
# systemd-oomd is its own package on Ubuntu (part of systemd on Arch); enable-services.sh enables it.
systemd-oomd
python3-gi
qrencode
qt6-image-formats-plugins
# rfkill: omarchy-bluetooth-power, -restart-bluetooth and -restart-wifi run it; Arch ships the
# command in util-linux, Ubuntu in its own rfkill package. Without it the bar's Bluetooth
# switch could not lift the soft block (2026-10-14).
rfkill
ripgrep
slurp
# swappy: the screenshot editor oaxaca-capture-screenshot's notification opens, in place of
# Omarchy's Tensaku, which has no Ubuntu build (2026-10-14)
swappy
# tmux: preinstalled again 2026-10-14, with Omarchy's config (config/tmux), theme sync and bindings
tmux
# snapd: the Snap Store, its socket enabled by the package, decided 2026-10-10 (step 4 had pinned it out)
snapd
socat
gnome-sushi
system-config-printer
tesseract-ocr
tesseract-ocr-eng
udiskie
# ufw: Ubuntu's default firewall tool, left exactly as Ubuntu ships it (installed, disabled)
ufw
# unattended-upgrades: Ubuntu desktops get daily security updates through it (it reaches a
# stock desktop as a Recommends of python3-software-properties, which Install-Recommends off
# would skip); its postinst writes 20auto-upgrades with the debconf default of enabled
unattended-upgrades
wireless-regdb
wl-clipboard
wtype
fonts-font-awesome
xdg-terminal-exec
yaru-theme-icon
# yt-dlp: the Download Video Chromium extension's native messaging host runs it
# (bin/omarchy-chromium-ytdlp-host); ffmpeg, which it merges streams with, is already in the image
yt-dlp
zbar-tools
playerctl
libxkbcommon-tools
xdg-utils
xdg-user-dirs
desktop-file-utils
libglib2.0-bin
dconf-cli
python3
perl
pciutils
usbutils
fuse3
fprintd
libpam-fprintd
libpam-u2f
pamu2fcfg
fido2-tools
libfido2-1
blueman
# Added beyond Omarchy's list: omarchy-pkg-repo-add and the browser installers
# fetch keys and debs with curl over https and convert armored keys with gpg;
# Ubuntu's minimal set installs neither curl nor, in every image, gpg.
curl
gpg
ca-certificates

# Dropped from Omarchy's Arch list, with the reason:
# aether, cliamp, herdr, omacalc, omacut, omawrite, tensaku, tobi-try, ttfx:
#   Omarchy's own OPR packages, no Ubuntu build
# asdcontrol: separate package
# chromium: Ubuntu's chromium is a snap placeholder and snapd is pinned out
# dotnet-runtime, ruby, lua51, luarocks, python-poetry-core: dev-env installs them via mise
# expac, fakeroot, pacman-contrib, yay: pacman tooling
# hyprland: the session layer packages it separately
# hyprland-preview-share-picker: separate package
# inetutils: not needed; hostname comes from hostname (essential)
# kernel-modules-hook: pacman hook, no apt counterpart
# lazydocker: not in stonking
# libyaml: runtime library comes in through its users' Depends
# localsend: separate package (not in stonking; the distro's apt repository builds it from LocalSend's release)
# mariadb-libs, postgresql-libs: come in through their users' Depends
# mise-bin: not in stonking; Install > Development > mise runs the vendor script
# moonlight-qt: not in stonking
# obsidian, pinta: not in stonking
# omarchy-nvim (nvim config): separate package
# qemu-user-static-binfmt: not needed
# quickshell, sddm, uwsm, wireplumber, gnome-keyring, xdg-desktop-portal*: session layer
# tldr: tealdeer ships /usr/bin/tldr
# ttf-ia-writer, ttf-jetbrains-mono-nerd-basic: separate fonts package
# tzupdate: not in stonking
# ufw-docker: not in stonking, and no longer needed: the port applies no ufw rules
# usage: mise installs it on demand
# zram-generator (systemd-zram-generator): not in Ubuntu's desktop seeds, and stonking's package
# creates a zram0 swap device from its own default config just by being installed; swap is the
# installer's (step 7), with Ubuntu's defaults
#
# Removed 2026-10-10 (the distro's split of the list into what the desktop needs and what was
# Omarchy's taste; see the distro repository's docs/6, "Core and apps"):
# libreoffice, kdenlive, obs-studio, xournalpp: Omarchy's removable preinstalls
# docker.io, docker-buildx, docker-compose-v2: Docker, with its scripts and binding
# build-essential, lazygit, btop, tree-sitter-cli, dua-cli, tealdeer, inxi,
#   starship, zoxide, eza: command-line preferences (yt-dlp, removed with them, came back on
#   2026-10-14 for the Download Video extension); the shell rc guards starship, zoxide and eza
# whois, unzip, wf-recorder, libsecret-tools: nothing in the port calls them
# gnome-disk-utility: the optional omarchy-desktop-apps package; evince: replaced by papers (above) (ffmpegthumbnailer is a dependency of omarchy-desktop)
